\chapter{Evaluation Methodology}
\label{ch:evaluation_methodology}
The preceding chapters have modeled the proposed extension FELICS from a purely theoretical standpoint: not only were the principles guiding its design established in Chapter~\ref{sec:conception}, but its behavior was also formalized and, in several cases, mathematically demonstrated (Chapter~\ref{sec:modeling_updated_metric}). However, it remains to be shown whether the metric behaves in practice as its theoretical construction suggests. To this end, a thorough evaluation is conducted against a reference implementation, in which several central research questions are addressed and the resulting data and findings are processed and visualized.

\section{Research Questions}
\label{sec:research_questions}

To empirically evaluate the efficacy, theoretical soundness, and practical implications of the proposed FELICS framework, the experimental design pursued in this chapter is guided by five research questions, each formulated as a testable hypothesis. Taken together, these hypotheses assess whether the implementation successfully fulfills the formalized requirements (see Section~\ref{sec:requirements}) and gauge which behaviors the metric extension exhibits in practice.

\begin{enumerate}[
		label=\textbf{Q\arabic*},
		ref=R\arabic*,
		wide=0pt,
		leftmargin=0pt
	]
	\item \label{rq:correlation_diff} \textbf{Impact of Interaction-Presumption Strength on Causal Effect Estimates}

	      \noindent\textit{Research question.} Are stronger oracle presumptions of concept interaction associated with larger differences in Causal Effect (CE) estimates when interactions are modeled explicitly?

	      \noindent\textit{Hypothesis.} As established in Section~\ref{sec:joint_counterfactual}, the oracle $\mathcal{O}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{C},n_{\max})$ partitions the concept set $\mathbf{C}$ of a question $\mathbf{x}$ into candidate interaction groups. Larger or more numerous groups indicate a stronger oracle presumption that concepts should be evaluated jointly; they do not constitute a direct measurement of statistical association or response-level interaction. Increasing $n_{\max}$ permits larger groups and therefore allows FELICS to examine more of the presumed interaction structure. It is hypothesized that the difference between a concept's atomic effect and its interaction-adjusted effect correlates positively with the corresponding difference in interaction-presumption strength. Since FELICS reduces exactly to the original Causal Concept Faithfulness metric whenever only singleton interventions are considered, as required by \reqref{req:one_item_group}, introduced in Section~\ref{sec:requirements}, this comparison can be conducted entirely within one joint FELICS run, without requiring a separate evaluation run of the original implementation of Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024}.

	\item \label{rq:convergence} \textbf{Convergence to the Baseline Metric}

	      \noindent\textit{Research question.} Does FELICS, when restricted to atomic interventions, converge to the original Causal Concept Faithfulness metric not only in theory but also in practically observed results?

	      \noindent\textit{Hypothesis.} Math Demo~\ref{ma:one_item_group} in Appendix~\ref{app:mathematical_demonstrations} establishes that whenever every candidate interaction group contains exactly one concept, the causal-effect formula of FELICS reduces exactly to that of the original metric of Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024}, thereby meeting the singleton-group compatibility criterion specified by \reqref{req:one_item_group}, introduced in Section~\ref{sec:requirements}. Because FELICS is nevertheless evaluated using a separate implementation, this theoretical equivalence must still be verified: discrepancies could arise from accidental changes that break compatibility. It is therefore hypothesized that, under identical cached inputs and random seeds, FELICS with $n_{\max}=1$ agrees numerically with the original implementation, and that independently sampled runs remain equivalent within prespecified practically negligible margins at both the CE and question-level faithfulness levels.

	\item \label{rq:ranking} \textbf{Preservation of Within-Question Concept Rankings}

	      \noindent\textit{Research question.} Does the payout-coefficient correction applied by FELICS preserve the relative ranking of concepts within a question, or does it reshuffle which concept appears most responsible for an unfaithful explanation?

	      \noindent\textit{Hypothesis.} \reqref{req:preserving_strengths}, introduced in Section~\ref{sec:requirements}, requires FELICS to preserve the ability of the metric of Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} to reveal interpretable, question-level patterns of unfaithfulness. This requirement extends beyond matching aggregate CE or faithfulness values: the corrected metric must continue to tell a coherent story about which concepts drive unfaithfulness within an individual question. It is hypothesized that, within a question, the rank order of concepts obtained from the adjusted causal effect $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}$ correlates strongly and positively with the rank order obtained from the raw, uncorrected Shapley causal effect $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{raw}}$---that is, the payout-coefficient correction rescales the magnitude of each concept's estimated effect without substantially reordering which concepts appear most responsible. Should this hypothesis be rejected, the resulting loss of per-question interpretability would remain a relevant cost to report, even if the aggregate equivalence targeted by \rqrefs{rq:convergence}{rq:faithfulness_increase} holds.

	\item \label{rq:faithfulness_increase} \textbf{Enhancement of Faithfulness Scores through Interaction Modeling}

	      \noindent\textit{Research question.} Does capturing interaction effects between concepts yield higher measured faithfulness for the evaluated models?

	      \noindent\textit{Hypothesis.} Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} report that most of the LLMs evaluated on their benchmark datasets already behave in a relatively faithful manner. However, because their original metric assumes disentangled, non-interacting concepts, it cannot capture cases in which a genuinely faithful model's explanation reflects the joint, rather than individual, influence of correlated concepts (cf.\ Section~\ref{sec:remaining_pipeline}). This mismatch artificially widens the gap between the Causal Effect and the Explanation-Implied Effect, thereby penalizing an otherwise faithful model with an unfairly low faithfulness score. Since FELICS is designed to close exactly this gap, and given that evaluated LLMs can generally be assumed to be relatively faithful, it is hypothesized that question-level faithfulness scores are significantly higher once interaction effects are considered ($n_{\max}>1$) than under the atomic baseline ($n_{\max}=1$). As with \rqref{rq:correlation_diff}, both quantities are obtained from one joint FELICS run, relying on the equivalence between FELICS restricted to atomic effects and the original metric of Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} targeted by \rqref{rq:convergence}.

	\item \label{rq:motivating_example} \textbf{Resolution of the Motivating Underestimation Example}

	      \noindent\textit{Research question.} Does FELICS resolve the specific underestimation identified as motivation for this thesis?

	      \noindent\textit{Hypothesis.} As discussed in Section~\ref{sec:pratical_limit}, Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} identify a MedQA question in which the concepts \textit{the patient's eating disorder} and \textit{the patient's self-perception of weight} each receive a surprisingly low Causal Concept Effect, because the evaluated LLM can reconstruct either concept from the other whenever only one of them is removed. Section~\ref{sec:interaction_effects} establishes that, under the removal-based intervention scheme adopted for this dataset, this behavior constitutes redundancy, characterized by $J_{\mathbf{x}}(\{A,B\}) > J_{\mathbf{x}}(\{A\}) + J_{\mathbf{x}}(\{B\})$ for the two concepts $A$ and $B$. Consequently, it is hypothesized that FELICS's grouped, corrected estimate exceeds the original estimate of Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} for both concepts, i.e., $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}(A) > \mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{Matton}}(A)$ and $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}(B) > \mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{Matton}}(B)$, and that this relationship holds consistently across all evaluated models.

\end{enumerate}

\section{Evaluation Settings}
The research questions are investigated under a common set of implementation and sampling decisions. The following subsections document the exact settings used to make the experiments reproducible and their results comparable.

\subsection{Reference Implementation}
\label{sec:reference_implementation}
Thus far, FELICS has only been modeled mathematically, without an accompanying executable implementation. However, an implementation is required to automatically gather empirical data and to allow fellow researchers to use the FELICS pipeline without having to reimplement it themselves. A \emph{reference implementation} was therefore developed, building largely upon the same code base as the original implementation by Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} and introducing changes only where appropriate and necessary. The repository, available at \url{https://github.com/JonathanZopf/FELICS}, is a fork of the upstream implementation of Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} at \url{https://github.com/kmatton/walk-the-talk}. Both experimental implementations are maintained in this fork: shared adaptations for local AI workstations and automated benchmarking preceded the separation into a baseline branch and a FELICS branch. Consequently, the baseline experiments use the adapted Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} implementation in the fork. Appendix~\ref{app:reproducibility} documents the experimental revisions, execution commands, and repository artifacts.

\subsection{Selection of Datasets}
The evaluation is conducted on three distinct datasets. BBQ and MedQA represent natural-language datasets with their own particular characteristics and enable a direct comparison between FELICS and the original metric of Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024}. New German Credit represents a sample tabular dataset, offering the advantages of reliable and deterministic concept extraction and association-based grouping (see Section~\ref{sec:oracle_tabular}). Because the original implementation does not natively support tabular data, it was extended with the required tabular input and intervention handling for the implementation comparison in \rqref{rq:convergence}.

\subsubsection{BBQ}
This English-language dataset was developed by Parrish et al.~\cite{parrishBBQHandbuiltBias2022} to expose hidden biases of LLMs against minorities and marginalized groups in a question-answering context. Typically, an ambiguous context with limited additional information is provided, prompting models to rely on hidden cues that often reflect stereotypes. The LLM is required to choose from three answer choices (Person A, Undetermined, Person B), although no objectively correct answer exists. Because this dataset is well suited for exposing biases in LLMs, it was also used by Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} in their own evaluation, making it particularly useful for comparing the behavior of FELICS to that of the original metric.

\subsubsection{MedQA}
Introduced by Jin et al.~\cite{jinWhatDiseaseDoes2021}, this dataset poses medical questions to the LLM, drawn from real licensing exams administered in Chinese and English. Like Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024}, this evaluation only makes use of the subset of English-language questions in which a hypothetical patient is described and the model must select the most likely diagnosis from a set of predetermined answer choices, based on the information provided. Unlike BBQ, MedQA therefore has a clear correct answer; however, this correctness is not used for evaluation, since it is more relevant whether the model's internal reasoning and explanation are aligned than whether its final choice happens to be factually correct.

\subsubsection{New German Credit}
The German Credit dataset and its updated version, South German Credit \cite{grompingSouthGermanCredit2019}, are widely used within the machine learning community. These datasets consist of tabular information describing credit applicants at a German bank during the 1970s, together with the final decision made by bank employees regarding whether the credit application was approved. One notable aspect of this data is the correlated nature of several columns. However, the outdated nature of both datasets---for instance, the use of the obsolete \emph{Deutsche Mark} currency, or telephone ownership as a recorded property---may confuse modern LLMs. An updated version inspired by the original dataset was therefore constructed specifically for this evaluation, using an algorithm that reduced the number of columns, made the recorded values more descriptive and up to date, and adjusted monetary amounts such as income to reflect current economic conditions in Germany, while still exhibiting a high degree of correlation among its columns. The resulting dataset is available in the FELICS source code, within the \path{/data} subfolder.

The general evaluation pipeline described in Section~\ref{sec:modeling_updated_metric} is applied uniformly across all three datasets: input values are modified to observe whether the model's final decision changes. As with the original metric by Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024}, the prompts used within FELICS still need to be adjusted individually for each dataset; these prompts can likewise be found in the source code, within the \path{/data} subfolder.

\subsection{Selection of Models}
Causal Concept Faithfulness, as proposed by Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024}, is designed to be compatible with an arbitrary LLM, a strength that FELICS preserves. Selecting concrete models for evaluation is therefore inherently difficult. This decision was guided by the constraint that a given model must either be hostable on local hardware or accessible via a free API with reasonable usage limits, owing to cost constraints. Based on these criteria, two medium-sized, state-of-the-art LLMs were selected.

Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} evaluate \texttt{GPT-3.5}, \texttt{GPT-4o}, and \texttt{Claude~3.5 Sonnet}. Although reusing these models would facilitate direct reproduction of their experimental setting, their proprietary, paid API access was not feasible within the resources available for this thesis; the evaluation therefore uses open-weight models that can be executed locally or accessed without comparable usage costs.

\subsubsection{gpt-oss:120b}
This LLM, introduced by OpenAI as an open-weights model in 2025, employs a mixture-of-experts architecture and comprises 117 billion parameters \cite{Gptoss2026}. According to its developer, its performance is comparable to that of the proprietary \texttt{GPT-4o-mini}. It serves as an example of a current American reasoning model and is executed on local infrastructure.

\subsubsection{Mistral Medium 3.5}
This model has a similar number of parameters (128 billion) but does not employ a \emph{mixture-of-experts} architecture \cite{MistralMedium35}. It was released by the French company Mistral in 2026 and is likewise available as an open-weights model with strong reasoning capabilities. The API provided by Mistral offers a generous usage policy, which helps conserve local computing resources.

\texttt{gpt-oss:120b} serves not only as a model under evaluation but also as the auxiliary LLM responsible for concept extraction from questions, interaction presumption, counterfactual generation, and concept extraction from the generated explanations for all runs of the experiment. This choice is motivated not only by its reliability but also by its similarity to \texttt{GPT-4o-mini}, which Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} used as their own auxiliary LLM.

\subsection{Samples per Question}
Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} collected 50 samples per original and counterfactual question in their own work. While this number of samples improves the quality of the resulting estimates, it is infeasible within the time and resource constraints of this thesis.

Therefore, for both models, \textbf{$S=10$} responses are gathered for BBQ and MedQA. Although this constitutes only a fifth of the sample size used by Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024}, it is sufficient to obtain meaningful results. For New German Credit, the number of samples can be further reduced to \textbf{$S=5$}, since each question is conceptually identical and differs only in its underlying data, meaning that fewer data points are required.

\subsection{Removal vs.\ Value-Replacement Counterfactuals}
For MedQA, Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} use counterfactuals in which the value of the concept under intervention is \emph{removed}. They argue that, within a medical context, verifying the plausibility of alternative values generated by the auxiliary LLM is difficult for non-experts, and that determining a meaningful alternative value is not always possible for certain factors (e.g., blood pressure). Conversely, for the experiments on the BBQ dataset, Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} use \emph{value replacements}, in which properties are typically swapped between the two persons described in the question.

Although it would be interesting to evaluate alternative intervention approaches, it has been decided, for the sake of comparability and given the limited available resources, that the evaluation is conducted using exactly the same counterfactual-generation approach as Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} for each respective dataset. The newly introduced New German Credit dataset is assessed using \emph{concept removal}, since the absence of potentially conflicting information (as discussed in Section~\ref{sec:influence}) may help to better reveal the redundant interaction effects of certain concepts.

\subsection{Explanations Used to Estimate Explanation-Implied Effects}
\label{sec:ee_evaluation_setup}
The source of the explanations used to estimate the explanation-implied-effect (EE) vector depends on the intervention strategy. For the removal-based datasets MedQA and New German Credit, the EE vector is estimated exclusively from explanations generated for the original, unmodified question. Explanations generated for removal counterfactuals are not included in this estimate. For BBQ, which uses value-replacement counterfactuals, explanations from both the original and counterfactual questions are used in accordance with Equation~\ref{eq:ee}.

This dataset-specific choice follows the experimental procedure reported by Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} for removal-based interventions. It is also required by the semantics of concept removal: once a concept has been removed from the question, a valid explanation of the resulting answer cannot generally be expected to identify that concept as influential. Including such counterfactual explanations would therefore reduce the estimated EE for reasons introduced by the intervention itself rather than because the original explanation failed to reflect the model's behavior. This concern is particularly pronounced for FELICS because a joint intervention may remove several concepts simultaneously, thereby mechanically restricting the set of concepts that can legitimately occur in the counterfactual explanation. Counterfactual responses remain necessary for estimating causal effects from changes in the answer distribution; the restriction applies only to the explanations used to construct the EE vector.

\section{Operationalization and Analysis Procedures}
\label{sec:analysis_procedures}

Whereas Section~\ref{sec:research_questions} specifies what each research question asks and states the corresponding hypothesis, this section operationalizes the relevant constructs and defines the quantitative or qualitative procedure used to assess each hypothesis under the experimental settings established above. Outputs from the Causal Concept Faithfulness metric by Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024} and from FELICS are analyzed in the statistical software \texttt{R}. The corresponding scripts can be found in the \path{/evaluation} subfolder of the FELICS repository introduced in Section~\ref{sec:reference_implementation}.

\subsection{Controlled Atomic Baseline from a Joint FELICS Run}
\label{sec:controlled_atomic_baseline}

Several analyses compare the interaction-aware formulation of FELICS with an atomic baseline. Both quantities are derived from the same joint FELICS run rather than from two independently sampled runs. This construction follows directly from the Shapley evaluation design. For each oracle group $s$, the joint run evaluates every nonempty treatment $T\subseteq s$ required by the marginal contributions in Equation~\ref{eq:ce_adj}. Consequently, it necessarily includes the singleton treatment $T=\{C_m\}$ for every concept $C_m\in s$. Applying the value function from Equation~\ref{eq:value_function} to this treatment yields the atomic causal effect
\begin{equation}
	\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{atomic}}(\mathbf{x},C_m)
	:=v_{\mathbf{x}}(\{C_m\}).
\end{equation}
If FELICS were restricted to $n_{\max}=1$, the oracle group containing $C_m$ would be $s=\{C_m\}$. The Shapley sum would then contain only the marginal contribution
\begin{equation}
	\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}(\mathbf{x},C_m,\{C_m\})
	=v_{\mathrm{adj},\mathbf{x}}(\{C_m\})-v_{\mathrm{adj},\mathbf{x}}(\emptyset)
	=v_{\mathbf{x}}(\{C_m\}),
\end{equation}
where the final equality follows from the singleton compatibility of the correction established in Math Demo~\ref{ma:one_item_group}. Thus, the singleton effects already evaluated as part of a joint run recover the CE estimator of an atomic FELICS configuration; model responses to treatments with $|T|>1$ do not enter $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{atomic}}$.

This reuse also provides a controlled comparison. For each question, the identified concept set, original-response samples, singleton-counterfactual samples, and all other preprocessing decisions are identical between the atomic and interaction-aware variants. Moreover, a single explanation-implied-effect vector $\mathbf{EE}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{C})$ is held fixed. The two paired faithfulness estimates are therefore
\begin{align}
	\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{atomic}}(\mathbf{x})
	 & :=\operatorname{PCC}\!\left(\mathbf{CE}_{\mathrm{atomic}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{C}),\mathbf{EE}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{C})\right), \\
	\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{joint}}(\mathbf{x})
	 & :=\operatorname{PCC}\!\left(\mathbf{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{C}),\mathbf{EE}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{C})\right).
\end{align}
Any difference between them is consequently attributable to the inclusion and attribution of joint causal effects rather than to stochastic variation in concept extraction, counterfactual generation, model responses, or EE estimation.

This construction should be distinguished from a complete independent rerun with $n_{\max}=1$. It reproduces the atomic CE estimator but deliberately retains the EE vector and sampled artifacts of the joint run. This is necessary to isolate the effect of interaction-aware CE estimation; an independently sampled atomic run would instead answer the separate implementation-equivalence question considered in \rqref{rq:convergence} and would introduce additional model stochasticity.

\subsection{Interaction-Presumption Strength and Causal-Effect Changes}
This subsection operationalizes \rqref{rq:correlation_diff}, introduced in Section~\ref{sec:research_questions}.

\noindent\textbf{Scope and comparison.} The relationship between the strength of the oracle's interaction presumption and the resulting CE difference is assessed separately for each evaluated model on the natural-language datasets $\mathbf{X} \in \{\text{BBQ}, \text{MedQA}\}$. A single joint FELICS run with $n_{\max}=4$ supplies the controlled atomic and interaction-aware quantities defined in Section~\ref{sec:controlled_atomic_baseline}.

\noindent\textbf{Operationalization.} For every question $\mathbf{x}$ with concept set $\mathbf{C}$, the interaction-presumption strength under configuration $n_{\max}$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
	\kappa(\mathbf{x}, n_{\max}) := \frac{1}{|\mathbf{C}|} \sum_{s \,\in\, \mathcal{O}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{C},n_{\max})} \ \sum_{\emptyset \neq T \subseteq s} |T|,
\end{equation}
that is, the total number of concept occurrences across all nonempty treatments evaluated for $\mathbf{x}$, normalized by the number of concepts $|\mathbf{C}|$. Thus, $\kappa$ summarizes the extent of joint treatment implied by the oracle partition; it is not an association coefficient and does not establish interaction in the model response. In the implementation, $|T|$ is obtained from the treatment's intervention bit vector. Since every candidate interaction group is a singleton under the atomic configuration, $\kappa(\mathbf{x},1)=1$ analytically for every question; hence only $\kappa(\mathbf{x},4)$ must be calculated from the joint run. The difference in interaction-presumption strength is denoted by $\Delta\kappa(\mathbf{x}) := |\kappa(\mathbf{x},4)-1|$.

For a concept $C_m \in \mathbf{C}$ and $C_m \in s$, let $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{atomic}}(\mathbf{x},C_m)$ denote the effect of the singleton treatment $\{C_m\}$, and let $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}(\mathbf{x},C_m)$ denote the adjusted Shapley effect allotted from the interventions on $s$. The primary question-level CE difference is then
\begin{equation}
	\Delta \mathrm{CE}(\mathbf{x}) := \frac{1}{|\mathbf{C}|}\sum_{C_m\in\mathbf{C}}
	\left|\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}(\mathbf{x},C_m)-\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{atomic}}(\mathbf{x},C_m)\right|.
\end{equation}
Taking the absolute difference before averaging prevents increases and decreases for different concepts from cancelling. As a sensitivity analysis, the absolute difference between the two question-level CE means is also reported.

\noindent\textbf{Statistical procedure.} A table with one row per question is assembled containing $\Delta\kappa(\mathbf{x})$ and $\Delta \mathrm{CE}(\mathbf{x})$. The relationship between both columns is assessed using a one-sided Pearson correlation test with the alternative hypothesis that the correlation is positive. The procedure is repeated independently for each dataset and model. To evaluate the analogous relationship for faithfulness, the Bayesian faithfulness pipeline is applied separately to $\mathbf{CE}_{\mathrm{atomic}}$ and $\mathbf{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}$, while holding $\mathbf{EE}$ fixed as specified in Section~\ref{sec:controlled_atomic_baseline}. The absolute difference between the resulting paired question-level scores, $\Delta\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x}):=|\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{joint}}(\mathbf{x})-\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{atomic}}(\mathbf{x})|$, is then correlated with $\Delta\kappa(\mathbf{x})$.

\noindent\textbf{Decision criterion.} Denoting the resulting Pearson correlation coefficient by $\mathrm{PCC}_{\Delta}$, the hypothesis is considered supported for a given dataset and model if $\mathrm{PCC}_{\Delta} \ge 0.10$ and $p \le 0.10$.

\noindent\textbf{Applicability.} Fixing the joint configuration to $n_{\max}=4$ for BBQ and MedQA prevents the oracle's ability to form larger candidate interaction groups from being unnecessarily restricted, while keeping $n_{\max}$ identical across all questions of a dataset ensures that $\Delta\kappa(\mathbf{x})$ remains comparable. The procedure cannot be applied to New German Credit because its oracle produces a single, dataset-wide partition of concepts (Section~\ref{sec:oracle_tabular}) rather than a per-question grouping. Consequently, $\kappa(\mathbf{x},4)$ would be identical for every question, leaving no variance in $\Delta\kappa(\mathbf{x})$ against which to correlate $\Delta \mathrm{CE}(\mathbf{x})$.

\subsection{Equivalence with the Original Metric}
This subsection operationalizes \rqref{rq:convergence}, introduced in Section~\ref{sec:research_questions}.

\noindent\textbf{Scope and comparison.} The convergence of FELICS with $n_{\max}=1$ to the original Causal Concept Faithfulness metric is evaluated separately for each model on BBQ, MedQA, and New German Credit. For New German Credit, the original implementation is extended to accept tabular inputs while retaining its singleton-effect estimation procedure. The outputs of both pipelines are restricted to the questions present in both. For each shared question $\mathbf{x}$, the mean CE across all its concepts is computed using $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{Matton}}(\mathbf{x},C_m)$ for the baseline and $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}(\mathbf{x},C_m,\{C_m\})$ for FELICS. The question-level faithfulness score $\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})$ is also obtained from each pipeline.

\noindent\textbf{Deterministic implementation-agreement check.} Where identical cached model outputs and identical random seeds are available for both pipelines, the maximum absolute difference, mean absolute difference, and proportion of paired CE values and question-level faithfulness scores equal within the numerical tolerance $\eta_{\mathrm{tol}}=10^{-8}$ are reported. Exact implementation agreement is established when the maximum absolute difference does not exceed $\eta_{\mathrm{tol}}$ and all pairs fall within this tolerance. For independently sampled runs or runs using different counterfactual inputs, these quantities remain descriptive diagnostics but cannot establish or refute deterministic implementation agreement.

\noindent\textbf{Secondary empirical equivalence test.} Independently sampled runs can differ even when both implementations realize the same estimand. Paired Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) therefore assess whether such differences remain practically negligible. For each shared question, the symmetric relative CE difference is
\begin{equation}
	d_{\mathrm{CE}}(\mathbf{x}) :=
	\frac{2\left(\overline{\mathrm{CE}}_{\mathrm{adj}}(\mathbf{x})-\overline{\mathrm{CE}}_{\mathrm{Matton}}(\mathbf{x})\right)}
	{\overline{\mathrm{CE}}_{\mathrm{adj}}(\mathbf{x})+\overline{\mathrm{CE}}_{\mathrm{Matton}}(\mathbf{x})+\eta_{\mathrm{tol}}},
\end{equation}
and the absolute-scale faithfulness difference is $d_{\mathcal{F}}(\mathbf{x}):=\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{FELICS}}(\mathbf{x})-\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{Matton}}(\mathbf{x})$. Prespecified equivalence margins of $\delta_{\mathrm{CE}}=0.1$ and $\delta_{\mathcal{F}}=0.1$ are used, corresponding to a symmetric relative CE difference of at most approximately $10\%$ and an absolute faithfulness-score difference of at most $0.1$.

\noindent\textbf{Decision criterion.} When the identical-input condition is met, deterministic compatibility is considered supported for a dataset and model if the implementation-agreement check succeeds for both CE and faithfulness. Empirical equivalence is reported as secondary evidence if both one-sided TOST $p$-values are below $0.05$ for $d_{\mathrm{CE}}$ within $[-\delta_{\mathrm{CE}},\delta_{\mathrm{CE}}]$ and for $d_{\mathcal{F}}$ within $[-\delta_{\mathcal{F}},\delta_{\mathcal{F}}]$.

\subsection{Preservation of Within-Question Concept Rankings}
This subsection operationalizes \rqref{rq:ranking}, introduced in Section~\ref{sec:research_questions}.

\noindent\textbf{Scope and comparison.} Rank preservation is evaluated for every model on all three datasets: BBQ, MedQA, and New German Credit. Both an atomic and a joint oracle configuration are considered. The atomic configuration $n_{\max}=1$ serves as a sanity check because $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{raw}}$ and $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}$ coincide trivially for singleton groups. The joint configuration uses $n_{\max}=4$ for BBQ and MedQA and $n_{\max}=3$ for New German Credit.

\noindent\textbf{Operationalization.} For every concept-level observation, the raw Shapley causal effect is defined as
\begin{equation}
	\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{raw}}(\mathbf{x},C_m,s) := \phi_m(v_{\mathbf{x}}),
\end{equation}
using the question-specific unadjusted value function $v_{\mathbf{x}}$ in the Shapley formulation of Equation~\ref{eq:shapley}. This value is paired with the adjusted Shapley causal effect $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}(\mathbf{x},C_m,s)$ from Equation~\ref{eq:ce_adj}.

\noindent\textbf{Statistical procedure.} The correspondence between the raw and adjusted causal effects is assessed using a one-sided Spearman rank-correlation test with the alternative hypothesis that the correlation is positive.

\noindent\textbf{Decision criterion.} The hypothesis is considered supported for a given dataset, model, and configuration if $\rho \ge 0.9$ and $p \le 0.05$.

\subsection{Effect of Interaction Modeling on Faithfulness}
This subsection operationalizes \rqref{rq:faithfulness_increase}, introduced in Section~\ref{sec:research_questions}.

\noindent\textbf{Scope and comparison.} The effect of modeling interactions on faithfulness is evaluated separately for every dataset and model using the controlled comparison defined in Section~\ref{sec:controlled_atomic_baseline}. The Bayesian faithfulness pipeline is applied to $\mathbf{CE}_{\mathrm{atomic}}$ and $\mathbf{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}$ while the explanation-implied-effect vector and sampled artifacts remain fixed. The joint configuration uses $n_{\max}=4$ for the natural-language datasets BBQ and MedQA and $n_{\max}=3$ for the tabular New German Credit dataset, consistent with the maximum group sizes established for the respective oracle implementations in Section~\ref{sec:joint_counterfactual}.

\noindent\textbf{Statistical procedure.} A paired, one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test is conducted with the alternative hypothesis that the interaction-aware faithfulness scores are stochastically greater than their atomic counterparts.

\noindent\textbf{Decision criterion.} The hypothesis is considered supported for a given dataset and model if the resulting $p$-value is $\le 0.05$.

\subsection{Resolution of the Motivating Underestimation Case}
This subsection operationalizes \rqref{rq:motivating_example}, introduced in Section~\ref{sec:research_questions}.

\noindent\textbf{Case-study design.} The identified MedQA question is run through both the original and the FELICS pipeline for each evaluated model. A table with the columns \emph{Concept}, $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{Matton}}$, and $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}}$ is constructed for every concept present in the question. The analysis then isolates the two concepts of interest: \textit{the patient's eating disorder} and \textit{the patient's self-perception of weight}.

\noindent\textbf{Assessment criterion.} The case is considered resolved if $\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{adj}} > \mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{Matton}}$ holds for both concepts and for every evaluated model. Because the hypothesis concerns one specific question rather than a distribution of questions, no inferential statistical test is applicable; the results are instead reported as a qualitative case study.
